  'body' => <<<'HTML'
<h2>What the Arts District Is</h2>
<p>The Washington Avenue Arts District sits just northwest of downtown Houston, near the Washington Avenue corridor and the rail lines that once served the area's warehouses and industrial buildings. Over the years many of those big, sturdy structures were converted into artist studios, galleries, creative offices and event spaces. Today the district is one of the largest concentrations of working artists in the city.</p>
<p>What makes it special is that it is a working district, not just a gallery row. Painters, sculptors, ceramicists, jewelers, photographers and designers make things here every day. On the right day, you can walk right in and see them at work.</p>

<h2>Silver Street Studios: Vintique Flea's Home</h2>
<p>Silver Street Studios, at 2000 Edwards Street, Houston, TX 77007, is one of the district's best-known studio buildings, and it has always been the home of Vintique Flea. The building's high ceilings, open floor and industrial bones make it a natural fit for a curated market of mid-century modern furniture, vintage clothing, <a href="/shop/architectural-salvage-industrial/">architectural salvage and industrial pieces</a>, rustic decor and antiques. There is something satisfying about shopping salvaged factory carts and old metal lockers inside a building that shares that same history.</p>
<p>Learn more about the market's story on our <a href="/about/">about page</a>, and check the <a href="/events/">events page</a> for upcoming dates.</p>

<h2>Open Studio Culture</h2>
<p>The district has a long tradition of open studio events, when artists open their doors to the public. These are the best times to visit if you want to see a lot of work in one go.</p>
<h3>How Open Studios Work</h3>
<ul>
<li>Artists who participate leave their studio doors open, often with work on the walls and a table of smaller pieces.</li>
<li>You are free to wander in, look around, ask questions and buy directly from the artist.</li>
<li>Some buildings hold events on a regular schedule, while others open their doors for special occasions. Check building or artist announcements for current schedules.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Studio Etiquette</h3>
<ol>
<li><strong>Say hello.</strong> Artists usually love talking about their process.</li>
<li><strong>Ask before photographing.</strong> Some artists are happy to have their work shared; others prefer not.</li>
<li><strong>Respect closed doors.</strong> A closed studio means the artist is not open to visitors right now.</li>
<li><strong>Do not haggle like you would at a flea.</strong> Art prices are usually set. If you love something out of reach, ask whether smaller works or prints are available.</li>
</ol>
<div class="tip"><strong>Tip:</strong> Buying straight from an artist is one of the best ways to build a collection you love. Many will tell you the story behind a piece, which makes it even better on your wall.</div>

<h2>Getting There and Parking</h2>
<p>The district is easy to reach from downtown, the Heights and the Washington Avenue corridor. Because it is built around warehouses, parking is generally more relaxed than in denser neighborhoods, but big events can fill lots quickly.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Arrive early</strong> on market and open studio days for the closest spots.</li>
<li><strong>Read the signs.</strong> Some lots are reserved for tenants or businesses, and towing is a real risk.</li>
<li><strong>Consider rideshare</strong> if you plan to enjoy beer and wine at the market.</li>
<li><strong>Bring a vehicle that fits your finds</strong> if you are shopping for furniture or salvage. See our <a href="/guides/how-to-shop-a-flea-market/">flea market tips</a> for getting big pieces home.</li>
</ul>
<p>Event-day parking details can change, so check the event listing or <a href="/contact/">contact us</a> before you go.</p>

<h2>Pairing a Visit With the Market</h2>
<p>A Vintique Flea weekend is a perfect excuse to explore the whole district. Here is an easy plan:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Start at the market</strong> when doors open. Serious furniture hunters know the best pieces go early.</li>
<li><strong>Grab lunch from the food trucks.</strong> Local food trucks have been part of every market. See our <a href="/food-trucks/">food trucks</a> page.</li>
<li><strong>Wander nearby studios</strong> if any are open, and look for art to go with the vintage piece you just bought.</li>
<li><strong>Circle back</strong> to the market before closing. Vendors sometimes have more flexibility on price late in the day.</li>
</ol>

<h2>What to Look For</h2>
<p>The district rewards curiosity. Look for original paintings and prints to hang above a <a href="/shop/mid-century-modern/">mid-century credenza</a>, handmade ceramics to pair with vintage barware, and small sculptures that work beautifully on a shelf of collectibles. Mixing new handmade art with vintage finds is one of the easiest ways to make a home feel personal. Our guide to <a href="/guides/mixing-vintage-patterns-textures/">mixing patterns and textures</a> has more ideas.</p>

<h2>Nearby Neighborhoods</h2>
<p>The arts district is a short drive from the Heights, with its walkable shopping around 19th Street, and from the Washington Avenue corridor, known for restaurants and nightlife. Buffalo Bayou's trails are also close by if you want to stretch your legs. For more ideas, see our <a href="/guides/houston-vintage-shopping-guide/">Houston vintage shopping guide</a>.</p>
